\par
The Duchess of Malfi has a more remarkable production history than almost any play of the early modern period. Its numerous adaptations and reworkings reveal not only the fascination the play has exerted over performers and directors, but also the drastically different meanings that it supports. The play has been variously produced as a character study of a paradoxical heroine, an exploration of political power and violence, an exercise in horror laced with poetry, and an existential parable about the meaninglessness of life in a godless universe. \par
\par
At the centre of the play is the figure of the Duchess, an incredibly demanding role for the boy actors who played the female roles in early modern theatre. Trying to project the range of emotion which the part demands must have been a very hard task for a youngster in his mid-teens, especially given how often the Duchess is the focus of attention on stage. From the intimate, playful and even flirtatious lines with Antonio in the early scenes, to her stoic facing up to death in prison, the part stretches across a wide emotional landscape. Bafflingly, she is then killed and is absent for the entire fifth climactic act of the play which bears her name. \par
\par
Changing the ending \par
\par
Striking productions have often come about when people tried to make sense of this confusing absence. In the 1730s, the literary scholar and poet Lewis Theobald wrote an adaptation of The Duchess of Malfi called The Fatal Secret, which attempted to reshape the play for more squeamish eighteenth-century audiences. The result is a play most modern readers would barely recognise. The Duchess and Antonio do not sleep together, no children are born, the Duchess stays alive throughout and only the Cardinal and Antonio die at the end. Virtue is triumphant, once the heroic characters have been made more clearly \lquote virtuous\rquote , according to the strict moral code of the era. The chaotic and bleak moral landscape of Webster\rquote s play, where \lquote integrity of life\rquote  (V.v.119) amid bloodshed and secrecy seems to be the best characters can hope for, is smoothed out into a simple diagram of good rewarded and wickedness punished. \par
\par
The Victorian actress Isabella Glyn solved the problem of the Duchess\rquote s absence in the final act by simply lopping that part off the end. She had become famous in a revival of The Duchess of Malfi staged at Sadler\rquote s Wells in 1850, and toured successfully with the play. Since she believed that the audience was coming mostly to see her, Glyn seems to have made a habit of cutting the last act. As far as she was concerned, the play was about the Duchess of Malfi, and there was no point it carrying on after there was no Duchess of Malfi left on the stage. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Without the fifth act, there is no love affair between Julia and Bosola, no eerie return of the Duchess in the echo scene, none of the emotional torment that makes Ferdinand believe he has become a werewolf and the Cardinal speculate about hell, no revenge mission for Bosola or final ironic bloodbath in which more than one person dies by accident, and no plan from Delio to rebuild the family by making the Duchess\rquote s son into a future Duke. Instead, there is a tragedy that follows the contours of the Duchess\rquote s life and imprisonment, ending with her death and Bosola\rquote s sudden fit of remorse. \par
\par
