\par
A minimalist Duchess \par
\par
Other productions have focused on the setting their director thinks the play should be performed within, and how that will shape meaning and audience interpretations. In the early 1890s William Poel and the Independent Theatre Society staged The Duchess of Malfi in a way that radically opposed the sort of stardriven mainstream theatre which Isabella Glyn\rquote s productions represented. Poel rejected the commercial theatre of his era and believed that the only way to understand theatre from Shakespeare\rquote s time was to produce it more or less as it had been performed back then. He argued for bare stages, minimal props and scenery, no lush orchestras or dramatic special effects and a focus on the original text as spoken by carefully trained actors in as authentic a way as possible. \par
\par
Picture \par
\par
Not a success \par
\par
This production was not a huge success. A particular controversy focused on a sequence in which Poel had substituted a \lquote Dance of Death\rquote  for the masque of madmen. This sequence, in which Ferdinand sends a group of lunatics to torment the Duchess, is often interpreted as a confusing and deranged dance, picking up on the play\rquote s concern with Ferdinand\rquote s madness and the more general lack of order and logic in the fictional world of Malfi. In Poel\rquote s version, it lost the madmen and involved young women of the court dancing to an atmospheric tom-tom accompaniment, before turning round to show they were dressed as skeletons on the other side. \par
\par
With its coupling of female beauty and death, this highlighted the emphasis in the play on the way in which mortality lurks beneath the surface of life, as seen in such moments as Bosola\rquote s sombre reflections to the Duchess during her imprisonment, or his railing against women who wear makeup. Critics of this production suggested the dance scene was artistically inconsistent, as it used melodramatic special effects and watered down the \lquote purity\rquote  of Poel\rquote s principles about performing only in bare, early modern style. Poel\rquote s supporters defended it as historically accurate, referring to imaginative paintings of the \lquote Dance of Death\rquote  by the sixteenth-century artist Holbein, while the director himself suggested that Italian court masques of the Duchess of Malfi\rquote s period were accustomed to perform such dances. \par
\par
A political Duchess \par
\par
In the 1980s the socialist theatre company Red Shift mounted another production with a definite agenda, though this time it was political rather than theatrical. They staged the play in modern dress, with all the characters in shades and black suits or black dresses, directing it in a chic, modern style, with overtones of film noir and gangster movies like The Godfather. This was intended to focus the audience\rquote s attention on the political aspects of the plot and the play\rquote s concern with the abuse of power by secretive organisations. It echoed Bosola\rquote s condemnation of the Cardinal who relies on spies, \lquote dooms men to death by information\rquote  and uses the law \lquote like a foul, black cobweb to a spider\'85To entangle those shall feed him\rquote  (I.i.57, 60, 62). A play like Malfi, which begins with a hitman returning from prison to demand that a Cardinal pay him off for keeping his mouth shut about who ordered the murders he committed, seemed to them to fit oddly well alongside modern gangster movies. \par
\par
The newspaper headlines at the time were full of the death of Robert Calvi, an Italian banker whose body had been found hanging underneath London Bridge. The investigation into his death had revealed networks of conspiracy and corruption involving the Freemasons, the Vatican and financial institutions. Because of this notorious case, Red Shift saw the opportunity to stage the play in a way that made the Italian location more than a vaguely exotic location for general skulduggery. They hoped to throw light on the elite groups who controlled institutions such as banks and political parties, causing their audiences to reflect on the unequal distribution of power in society, and the secretive way it is wielded. \par
\par
Political ends \par
\par
For this production, the court of Malfi was not just a glamorous backdrop, but a site of government. \lquote Duke\rquote  and \lquote Cardinal\rquote  were not just archaic titles, but positions of power in major institutions. There is support for their interpretation in the comments of the pilgrims on the rituals being carried out in Loretto in Act III, Scene iv: the repetition of words like \lquote free\rquote , \lquote state\rquote  and \lquote prince\rquote  suggesting the intention that the audience appreciate how religious and ceremonial power are being pressed into service for political ends. As one asks \lquote by what justice\rquote  the Duchess is being banished, the other replies \lquote Sure, I think by none / Only her brother\rquote s instigation\rquote  (III.iv.22\f1\endash 23). \par
\par
Red Shift\rquote s sartorial style and gangster atmosphere were probably more influential than their political analysis: many productions in the 1990s and onwards used suits and film noir aesthetics in their presentation of the play, but not many were aimed at a Marxist interpretation of society. This may be because film history overtook them: for most twenty-first century audiences, revenge tragedy performed in suits and shades is more likely to recall Reservoir Dogs and its nihilistic violence than the European film noir atmosphere that Red Shift were attempting. Even within such a short space of time, the same costumes and the same visual language came to mean rather different things to audiences. \par
\par
An interactive Duchess \par
\par
In the twenty-first century the experimental group Punchdrunk made the play into one of their off-beat interactive theatrical experiences. They saw the chaos, visceral drama and moral ambiguity in the text of The Duchess of Malfi as a starting point to produce an immersive production which drew audiences right into the terrifying world of the Malfi court. The literal darkness of the plot, within which Bosola kills Antonio by mistake and the Duchess thinks she is holding Ferdinand\rquote s hand when he has given her Antonio\rquote s severed one, suggests an element of chance and confusion in human life. Its dubious moral world, in which Bosola can be both a paid spy and the instrument of righteous revenge, denies the possibility of the audience standing back and making clear, simple ethical judgements. \par
\par
Punchdrunk prepared a production in an abandoned warehouse in London, in which audiences did not sit in seats in front of a stage, but were set loose to wander through the building, stumbling upon scenes of the play being performed in different parts of it. Rather than presenting the audience with a straightforward, linear telling of the story, Punchdrunk wanted them to find their own way through disjointed theatrical spectacles, making their own sense of what was going on around them. Producing the play in this way de-emphasised character development or political analysis, since the company could not be sure in what order any given member of the audience might see particular scenes or what sense they would make of them. It stressed the tone and atmosphere that pervaded Webster\rquote s fictional world, the sphere of paranoia, chance and uncertainty which the characters seem to inhabit. \par
\par
New fixations \par
\par
As all these productions demonstrate, theatrical artists have found The Duchess of Malfi a rich source of inspiration and challenge. Reading the play has suggested radically different meanings to different actors and directors. With Webster\rquote s tragedy now one of the handful of most-performed early modern works, it seems likely that new generations of directors will fixate on aspects of the play that are totally invisible to us at the moment. One day Red Shift\rquote s gangster politics and Punchdrunk\rquote s immersive chaos may seem as quaint and misguided as Lewis Theobald\rquote s sexless, deathless fairy tale. \par
